From 735f00e2574be3199a24a169c1f7e24deadab67d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bench <bench@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 03:33:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/76] perf(eccvm): narrow rows and zero-tail extension for
 grand product and log-derivative inverses

The ECCVM grand product and lookup-inverse computations fetched all 119 columns per row via get_row
and evaluated every one of the 2^15 fixed-size rows, although only 47 / 21 columns are read and every
row past the active trace (~6.5k rows on real flows) reads zeros.

- ECCVMFlavor lists the entities read by ECCVMSetRelation's numerator/denominator and by
  ECCVMLookupRelation's inverse computation (the short-monomial relations read the same ones);
  ProverPolynomials gains get_row_for_grand_product (picked up by the existing dispatch in
  compute_grand_product) and get_row_for_logderivative_inverse, plus get_grand_product_zero_row_start
  (first row from which all grand product columns are zero).
- ECCVMProver::compute_grand_product runs the generic grand product only up to that row
  (size_override) and extends z_perm over the zero rows by their constant numerator/denominator ratio,
  z_perm[i] = z_perm[i-1] * r, which is the same field element.
- The log-derivative inverses are computed through ECCVMFlavor::LogDerivativeInverseView, whose
  get_row is the narrow row.

No change to the generic honk libraries. Tests: NarrowRowGettersMatchFullRow (all rows, full and short
relations) and ProverGrandProductAndInversesMatchGeneric.
